"source_explanation": "いたい is written 痛い because 痛 means \"painful / hurting,\" which describes a headache.\n痛い (いたい) is an i-adjective meaning \"painful\" or \"it hurts.\" The kanji 痛 uses the kun-yomi いた in adjective form. The sentence says: \"Because my head 頭 (あたま) hurts 痛い (いたい), I took medicine.\" 頭が痛い (あたまがいたい) = \"my head hurts\" is one of the most common expressions used when describing illness or discomfort in Japanese. The on-yomi of 痛 is つう, appearing in 苦痛 (くつう, suffering/pain), 激痛 (げきつう, severe pain), and 鎮痛剤 (ちんつうざい, painkiller). The radical of 痛 is 疒 (illness/sickness radical), consistently used in health-relat"
